WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.314
A relational schema expresses entities, keys, relationships, and constraints.

00:00:05.464 --> 00:00:09.084
A transaction groups changes that must commit or roll back together.

00:00:09.234 --> 00:00:14.442
Isolation controls what concurrent transactions can observe; it does not automatically enforce

00:00:14.442 --> 00:00:15.744
every business rule.

00:00:15.894 --> 00:00:21.186
A read-then-write sequence can race unless protected by an appropriate lock, version

00:00:21.186 --> 00:00:23.391
check, conditional update, or constraint.

00:00:23.541 --> 00:00:27.686
Indexes speed selected reads and constraint checks but cost storage and write

00:00:27.686 --> 00:00:28.031
work.

00:00:28.181 --> 00:00:33.166
Design them for actual query predicates and ordering, then inspect execution plans

00:00:33.166 --> 00:00:34.412
on representative data.

00:00:34.562 --> 00:00:38.495
Avoid assuming that adding an index to every column improves performance.

00:00:38.645 --> 00:00:42.695
Keep transactions short to reduce lock duration and connection occupancy.

00:00:42.845 --> 00:00:45.712
The inventory schema has products and reservations.

00:00:45.862 --> 00:00:50.922
Stock cannot be negative, reservation quantity must be positive, and request_key is

00:00:50.922 --> 00:00:51.344
unique.

00:00:51.494 --> 00:00:55.614
The order schema records orders and outbox events in a separate database.

00:00:55.764 --> 00:00:59.959
An order ID in the inventory request is a business reference, not

00:00:59.959 --> 00:01:01.357
a cross-database foreign key.

00:01:01.507 --> 00:01:05.284
Design an index for finding a user’s newest orders and explain the

00:01:05.284 --> 00:01:06.229
ordering of columns.

00:01:06.379 --> 00:01:10.460
Write a transaction that updates stock and inserts a reservation, then force

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.841
the insert to fail and verify rollback.

00:01:12.991 --> 00:01:17.226
A candidate index begins with the equality-filtered user or tenant columns and

00:01:17.226 --> 00:01:20.755
continues with the time and stable tie-breaker used for ordering.

00:01:20.905 --> 00:01:23.696
Verify the plan with realistic cardinality.

00:01:23.846 --> 00:01:28.465
The failed reservation insert must leave stock unchanged because both changes share

00:01:28.465 --> 00:01:29.234
one transaction.

00:01:29.384 --> 00:01:30.056
Question.

00:01:30.206 --> 00:01:33.149
Does a transaction automatically prevent overselling?

00:01:33.299 --> 00:01:33.876
Answer.

00:01:34.026 --> 00:01:34.509
No.

00:01:34.659 --> 00:01:39.374
The isolation level and read-write pattern must enforce the stock invariant under

00:01:39.374 --> 00:01:39.767
concurrency.

00:01:39.917 --> 00:01:40.588
Question.

00:01:40.738 --> 00:01:42.911
Why can an unused index be harmful?

00:01:43.061 --> 00:01:43.638
Answer.

00:01:43.788 --> 00:01:48.903
It consumes space and increases insert, update, delete, and maintenance work without

00:01:48.903 --> 00:01:50.182
helping the workload.
